//? Title: PixelCNN: A Causal Binary Image Density
//? Subtitle: Structural masks, explicit teacher forcing and a visible raster sampler
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An image density can predict each pixel from the pixels before it in raster
order. A masked convolution enforces this restriction structurally: its current
and future input derivatives vanish exactly. This compact native C++ PixelCNN
uses four width-eight ReLU layers,1841 stored values and a Bernoulli head on
binary8x8 MNIST images. Worked masks and likelihoods precede the real data.
The complete program exposes all96 Adam updates, all64 sampling steps, the
full logit/input Jacobian and prefix completion. Its fixed three-epoch test
result is.6165 bits per binary pixel, worse than the fit-only independent
Bernoulli baseline's.3844. Seeded samples illustrate the density protocol;
they do not establish realistic generation or superior likelihood.
)
\toc

> Factor the image into causal Bernoulli predictions

[van den Oord, Kalchbrenner and Kavukcuoglu, Pixel Recurrent Neural Networks
(2016)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.06759) introduced image densities with explicit
pixel dependencies, including masked convolutional networks. This teaching
variant is a small binary PixelCNN: one gray channel, four biased3x3 SAME
stride-one convolutions of width8, ReLU after each, and a biased1x1 output.
It has no normalization, pooling, dropout, class conditioning or batch coupling.

For a binary8x8 image x, number the64 sites left-to-right, top-to-bottom. The
model predicts p_j=sigmoid(z_j(x_<j)) and assigns

$$ p_\theta(x)=\prod_{j=0}^{63}p_j^{x_j}(1-p_j)^{1-x_j}. $$
\alttext(The image probability is the product of64 Bernoulli conditional probabilities, each depending only on earlier raster sites.)

During fitting, all true preceding pixels are supplied together: teacher forcing
computes the64 conditionals in one network pass. During generation, each new
binary draw becomes input to the following pass. The restriction must hold in
the network, rather than relying on unknown pixels being filled with zeros.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pc_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(42,1500);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;
 s.width=9;s.height=4.6;s.font_size=35;
 d.node("x",{-14,5},"Binary image canvas\nOnly earlier pixels",s)
  .node("a",{0,5},"Mask A + ReLU\nExclude current input",s)
  .node("b",{14,5},"Three mask B + ReLU\nCausal hidden features",s)
  .node("p",{14,-5},"1x1 head + sigmoid\nNext Bernoulli p",s)
  .node("u",{0,-5},"Explicit uniform bank\nDraw x = [u < p]",s)
  .node("loop",{-14,-5},"Write one raster site\nPreserve any prefix",s)
  .edge("x","a").edge("a","b").edge("b","p")
  .edge("p","u").edge("u","loop").edge("loop","x")
  .save_svg("build/pc_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/pc_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Teacher forcing evaluates every conditional at once. The visible sampling loop instead visits64 sites in raster order, draws from an explicit uniform bank and feeds each new bit into the next pass.)
\alttext(A binary canvas passes through mask A, three mask B layers and a Bernoulli head. A uniform comparison writes one site and returns to the canvas.)

> Hand-check masks A and B before any fitting

A3x3 kernel reads its top row and its middle-left cell. Mask A excludes the
center, so the raw current pixel cannot enter its own prediction. Mask B also
allows the center hidden feature, which already depends only on past raw
pixels. Multiplying W by the mask inside the graph leaves forbidden stored
coordinates present but gives them exact zero partials.

For a single input and eight output channels, A contains32 ones and40 zeros.
Each8→8 mask B contains320 ones and256 zeros. Three B layers therefore leave
40+3*256=808 masked kernel coordinates. Including all biases and the1x1 head
there are1841 stored,1033 active values in10 leaves. Allowing a raw center
pixel would leak its target; setting unknown pixels to zero during generation
would not repair that training density.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pc_masks.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;namespace m=n::models;
 auto a=m::CausalPixelMask(1,1,false),b=m::CausalPixelMask(1,1,true);
 auto panel_a=n::image_grid(n::reshape(a,{9,1,1,1}),3,3,.5,45);
 auto panel_b=n::image_grid(n::reshape(b,{9,1,1,1}),3,3,.5,45);
 n::save_image_grid(n::stack({panel_a,panel_b}),"build/pc_masks.png",2,0,1,12,.5,1);
 std::print("Mask A: {}\nMask B: {}\n",n::reshape(a,{3,3}).values_str(),n::reshape(b,{3,3}).values_str());
}
```
