\image(figures/pc_completions.png)
// result_end
\caption(Eight rows show truth, observed prefix and sampled completion. Gray marks the32 unknown display pixels in the middle column. Each completion preserves its32 observed binary pixels exactly.)
\alttext(Eight completion cases each show the true binary image, its observed top half with a gray unknown bottom half, and the sampled completion.)

> Interpret a density result with an appropriate baseline

The independent baseline fits each site's Bernoulli parameter only on the1000
training images: q_j=(count_j+.5)/1001. It evaluates test log likelihood with
that **unrounded Double posterior**, while its archived probability image is
Float32. Using the rounded archive to recompute the scalar would alter the
original numerical protocol. Shared blank-border structure makes a site-specific
baseline useful here; learning conditional structure does not guarantee beating
it in96 updates.

The run restores all10 leaves exactly, repeats seeded images/uniforms/conditionals
bit for bit, preserves every completion prefix and leaves fitted parameters
unchanged during inference. A separate development oracle checks498099 forward
values,180418 analytic partials,28 scalar finite differences and all96 Adam
writes against the unchanged original. It independently reproduces5376 sampled
pixels across epoch snapshots, final samples and completions. Native and report
replacement arrays must match before the old executable is retired.

This experiment tests a compact causal density and its state protocol. It does
not measure classification accuracy, natural-image FID, perceptual realism or
full-paper PixelCNN performance. The fixed baseline comparison and the retained
blind spot are part of the result.
